   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the civil service law, in relation to provisional employees of\ncertain public employers; to amend chapter 5 of the laws of 2008 amending the\ncivil service law relating to provisional employees of certain public\nemployers, in relation to extensions of certain negotiated agreements and\nextending the provisions of such chapter; and to amend part I of chapter 56 of\nthe laws of 2008 amending the civil service law relating to excess provisional\nemployees of a city having a population of one million or more, in relation to\nextending the provisions thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S7100",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7100-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §65, Civ Serv L; amd §§3 & 5, Chap 5 of 2008; amd Part I §2, Chap 56 of 2008",
    "lawSection": "Civil Service Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7100\n\nSPONSOR: GOUNARDES\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the civil service law, in relation to provisional\nemployees of certain public employers; to amend chapter 5 of the laws of\n2008 amending the civil service law relating to provisional employees of\ncertain public employers, in relation to extensions of certain negoti-\nated agreements and extending the provisions of such chapter; and to\namend part I of chapter 56 of the laws of 2008 amending the civil\nservice law relating to excess provisional employees of a city having a\npopulation of one million or more, in relation to extending the\nprovisions thereof\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill sets forth legislative findings describing the\nneed to amend subdivision 5 of Civil Service Law § 65, added by Chapter\n5 of the Laws of 2008. Subdivision 5 provides for the City of New York\n(the \"City\") and the other governmental entities whose civil service\nappointments are managed by the City's Department of Citywide Adminis-\ntrative Services (\"DCAS,\" and such entities are referred to as the \"DCAS\nemployers\") to develop a plan to substantially comply with the time\nperiods permitted by subdivisions one through four of Civil Service Law\n§ 65 regarding provisional appointments.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend Civil Service Law § 65(5) to authorize\nthe DCAS employers to administer a civil service examination exclusively\nto incumbent employees who, at the time of application to take the exam-\nination, have served provisionally in that title for two or more years.\nIf the employee taking the \"qualified incumbent examination\" meets the\nminimum educational and other requirements set forth in the notice of\nthe examination, as established by the DCAS employers, the DCAS employ-\ners would be authorized to appoint permanently an employee with two or\nmore years of provisional service from the list resulting from the exam-\nination.  The titles for which DCAS could administer such a test to\nincumbent employees would be limited to those expressly identified in\nSection 4 of Chapter 467 of the Laws of 2016, Section 5 of Chapter 419\nof the Laws of 2018, and Section 5 of this bill, and DCAS would be\nauthorized to administer such a test no more than once per title.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would add a new paragraph (c-5) to Civil Service\nLaw § 65(5) authorizing the DCAS employers to submit to the State Civil\nService Commission (\"State Commission\") a revised plan, to be imple-\nmented by December 31, 2023, to further reduce the number of provisional\nappointments that have continued beyond the time periods permitted by §\n65(1)-(4). This plan could contain any of the components of the original\nplan and could also include other appropriate means of implementation.\nThe State Commission would be required to approve or disapprove the plan\nwithin sixty days of its submission, and this approval process would\notherwise conform to the procedures set forth in § 65(5)(c).\n\nSection 3 would also redefine the term \"substantial compliance\" as used\nin paragraph (b) of § 65(5) in light of the shrinking municipal work-\nforce in New York City and the challenges presented by recent develop-\nments described in the legislative findings. To achieve substantial\ncompliance, the DCAS employers would be required to reduce the number of\nprovisional appointments that have continued beyond the periods other-\nwise permitted by this section to 6.5% of the entire competitive class\nworkforce.\n\nSection 4 of the bill would make conforming amendments to paragraphs\n(d), (f), (g) and (h) of Civil Service Law § 65(5) in light of the\nproposed addition of a new paragraph (c-5) in Section 3 of the bill.\nThis would ensure that the plan modification procedures, which permit an\nextension of no more than one year, as well as the time limitation\nsuspension and authorization of negotiated disciplinary procedures,\nwould continue to apply.\n\nSection 5 of the bill sets forth the list of 33 titles for which DCAS\nwould be authorized to administer a qualified incumbent examination\npursuant to amended subparagraph (ii) of paragraph (c-2), in addition to\nthose titles authorized by Chapter 467 of the Laws of 2016 and Chapter\n419 of the Laws of 2018.\n\nSection 6 of the bill would make a conforming amendment to § 3 of Chap-\nter 5 of the Laws of 2008 with respect to the negotiated agreements\nentered into as a result of the plan, reflecting the amendment to para-\ngraph (g) of § 65(5) proposed by Section 4 of this bill. It would also\namend the expiration date of Chapter 5 of the Laws of 2008, which\nauthorized the original plan, and provide that this act would expire on\nDecember 31, 2023.\n\nSection 7 of the bill would amend the expiration date of Part I of Chap-\nter 56 of the Laws of 2008 (to December 31, 2023), which added subdivi-\nsion 6 of § 65, authorizing the State Commission to charge the City for\nservices and costs (up to $600,000 annually) associated with approving\nand monitoring the plan submitted by the City and the DCAS employers.\n\nSection 8 of the bill is a severability clause.\n\nSection 9 of the bill provides that the act would take effect immediate-\nly.\n\n \nREASONS FOR SUPPORT:\n\nThe purpose of this bill, and the 2008 legislation it amends, is to\naddress issues raised by the New York Court of Appeals in its decision\nin City of Long Beach v. Civil Service Employees Association, 8 N.Y.3d\n465 (2007). In that case, the Court concluded that the terms of a\ncollective bargaining agreement that afforded tenure rights to provi-\nsional employees of the City of Long Beach after one year of service\nwere contrary to New York statutory and decisional law., The Court also\nemphasized the need for compliance with the time limitations imposed by\nCivil Service Law § 65 with respect to filling vacancies, holding exam-\ninations, and removing provisional employees from positions in titles\nfor which examinations should be held.\n\nHaving received approval in 2008 from the State Commission to implement\nan orderly and expeditious plan to reduce the number of provisional\nemployees, the City and certain other related employers made substantial\nprogress toward their goal through various means authorized by Chapter\n5. As of December 31, 2018, the number of provisional employees on the\npayrolls of the DCAS employers was approximately 16,821, a substantial\nreduction of more than 20,000 from the number in May 2008, and the pace\nof provisional reduction had increased markedly. By increasing its\ncapacity to administer competitive examinations through the use of auto-\nmated examination development systems and other measures, during 2019\nand early 2020 the City reduced the number of provisional employees by\nmore than 10%, and in addition, the administration of qualified incum-\nbent examinations and other competitive civil service examinations led\nto a reduction in the number of long-term provisional appointees to 6.0%\nof all competitive class appointees. The City was close to achieving\nsubstantial compliance with the length of provisional appointments\npermitted by § 65 of the Civil Service Law.\n\nAlthough the number of provisionals has continued to steadily decrease,\nreaching 13,873 on February 28, 2021, provisionals serving beyond the\nstatutory time limit as a proportion of all competitive class appoint-\nments has slowly increased as a result of the COVID-19 pandemic. The\nincrease is not due to a large increase in the number of provisional\nappointments that continue beyond statutory time limits Rather, the\npandemic has required a hiring freeze and has interfered with the abili-\nty of DCAS to administer civil service tests. As a result, agencies have\nnot been able to replace provisionals serving for longer than nine\nmonths at the pace previously achieved. At the same time, there has been\na very significant decrease in the competitive class headcount the\ndenominator of the ratio - of nearly 10,000 by February 28, 2021.\n\nEven though the provisional headcount has continued to diminish, due to\nthe rapid decrease in the size of the work force, the proportion of\nprovisional appointments extending beyond the nine-month limit has\nincreased from a low of 6.0% in March 2020 to 7.1% as of February 28,\n2021. The bill would respond to the dramatic decrease in the size of the\nworkforce by adjusting the statutory definition of substantial com\npliance. Substantial compliance would be achieved when the proportion of\nprovisional appointments that continue beyond statutory time limits\nconstitute 6.5% or less of competitive class appointments. The existing\ntarget is 5%.\n\nThis bill would allow the DCAS employers to update and continue to\nimplement a plan under § 65(5), with appropriate revisions to timeframes\nand plan strategies. The bill would ensure the continued quality and\neffectiveness of governmental operations. At the same time it would\nfurther the goals of maintaining continuity in the provision of essen-\ntial public services and harmonious labor relations, particularly as it\nwould allow limited negotiated protections regarding disciplinary proce-\ndures for provisional employees and the waiver of time limitations that\nwould otherwise apply in relation to provisional employment. Such limit-\ned authorizations are consistent with the \"merit and fitness\" policy of\nthe State Constitution.\n\nAccordingly, the Mayor urges the earliest possible favorable consider-\nation of this proposal by the Legislature.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
